WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.150
From a public dataset to a shareable map story, in a few minutes.

2
00:00:06.545 --> 00:00:12.855
Everything in Vizzie lives in a project: the map, the charts, and the story you tell with them.

3
00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:25.970
Start with a place. Vizzie finds the government portals that publish data about it.

4
00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:41.390
Census maps are one click away. To go further, search every portal at once, or pick one you trust. Here, the Digital Atlas of Australia.

5
00:00:41.892 --> 00:00:48.272
Search for regional population. Results are datasets, and opening one goes straight to its preview.

6
00:00:53.459 --> 00:01:02.979
This is the table exactly as the ABS publishes it: seventy-nine columns, two thousand four hundred and fifty-four areas, and no boundaries.

7
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:11.530
The table has no shapes of its own. Vizzie recognised the SA2 codes, and matched every row to the official boundaries.

8
00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.940
One click on Viz it gives you a first draft: the map and its legend.

9
00:01:27.346 --> 00:01:36.586
Open the layer to restyle it. The same boundaries tell different stories. Density shows where people are. Change shows where they're arriving.

10
00:01:46.335 --> 00:01:52.265
Colour in segments to make the map readable at a glance. Spread decides where the breaks fall.

11
00:02:03.616 --> 00:02:10.826
The basemap can change too. Switch to satellite imagery, and the same data sits over the real landscape.

12
00:02:19.584 --> 00:02:26.284
Pick the columns you care about, and charts are offered as answers to questions, each with a live preview.

13
00:02:42.497 --> 00:02:47.217
Filter the charts to the map view, and they re-rank for the place you're looking at.

14
00:02:53.931 --> 00:03:00.151
Sketch layers let you mark up exactly what you want people to see. They stay pinned to the map as you zoom.

15
00:03:32.865 --> 00:03:41.165
A frame saves the view, the layers that are on, the charts, and the legend. Put frames in order, and you have a story.

16
00:04:02.122 --> 00:04:05.572
Present turns your frames into a slideshow.

17
00:04:15.284 --> 00:04:23.584
Publish it as an interactive presentation. Anyone with the link can open it, with no account, and the data credit stays attached.

18
00:04:38.154 --> 00:04:41.914
Vizzie. Free during beta, at vizzie.org.
